  "avoidBefore": "all",
  "energy": "Each dive is a single maximal take-off and a flight of 1-3 s, so it is almost all ATP-PC; the aerobic demand is low, but sessions hold many repeats.",
  "summary": "Diving needs a powerful take-off, fast rotation from a stiff, strong trunk, and wrists, shoulders and lower back that tolerate hundreds of water entries. The plan builds jump power and leg strength, anti-extension trunk strength, and shoulder, wrist and back resilience, kept short around pool work.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "A systematic review of 28 studies found the shoulder, lower back, trunk and wrist are the most injured areas in divers; low back pain is reported by up to 89% over a career.",
      "source": "Currie et al. 2025, Sports Health",
      "url": "https://researchsystem.canberra.edu.au/ws/portalfiles/portal/113004481/currie-et-al-2024-diving-into-the-health-problems-of-competitive-divers-a-systematic-review-of-injuries-and-illnesses.pdf",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Water entry was the mechanism in about 71% of reported injuries; platform divers mostly hurt the upper limb and springboard divers the lower limb.",
      "source": "Currie et al. 2025, Sports Health",
      "url": "https://researchsystem.canberra.edu.au/ws/portalfiles/portal/113004481/currie-et-al-2024-diving-into-the-health-problems-of-competitive-divers-a-systematic-review-of-injuries-and-illnesses.pdf",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Back and reverse dives, and platform more than springboard, are linked to more injuries; the wrist, shoulder and lumbar spine are injured most.",
      "source": "Rubin 1999, Clinics in Sports Medicine",
      "url": "https://lida.sport-iat.de/ta/Record/4002912?lng=en",
      "grade": "practice"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "limited",
